\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $K/k$ and $L/l$ be function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from $\ell$. Does the natural map from field isomorphisms, modulo Frobenius in positive characteristic, to Milnor-$K$-theory-preserving isomorphisms $K^*/(K^*)^\ell\to L^*/(L^*)^\ell$ give a bijection?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2014/49}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $K/k$ and $L/l$ be function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from $\ell$. Does the natural map from field isomorphisms, modulo Frobenius in positive characteristic, to Milnor-$K$-theory-preserving isomorphisms $K^*/(K^*)^\ell\to L^*/(L^*)^\ell$ give a bijection?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30002716 (OWR-13351-016). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2014/49}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30002716}

